WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
A neutron that leaves the nucleus and is left on its own decays within about fifteen minutes.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:14.340
But measure that span by two different methods and you get two different answers — and the eight seconds between them cannot be blamed on experimental error.

3
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:21.510
For twenty years, either someone misunderstands their own apparatus, or some of the neutrons are quietly going somewhere else.

4
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:37.840
The neutrons sitting right now in the nuclei of the atoms of your body have stood there, undecayed, since the first three minutes of the universe.

5
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.330
Thirteen point eight billion years.

6
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:48.180
Take one of those same neutrons out of the nucleus, leave it alone in an empty room, and it dies within a quarter of an hour.

7
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.550
It breaks apart, it scatters, it turns into a proton, an electron and an antineutrino.

8
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.480
Immortal inside the nucleus, mortal outside it.

9
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:08.090
The whole difference lies in the presence of its neighbours: the binding energy inside the nucleus does not leave it the tiny budget it needs in order to decay.

10
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.580
That budget really is tiny.

11
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:19.070
The mass of a free neutron, put in units of energy, is nine hundred thirty-nine point five six five megaelectronvolts.

12
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:27.040
The combined mass of the proton and the electron it leaves behind when it decays is nine hundred thirty-eight point seven eight three.

13
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.770
The entire affair takes place in the zero point seven eight two of space between those two numbers.

14
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:43.300
The neutron can decay because it fits into that thin energy gap between itself and its own products; close the gap, and there would be no such temporary thing in the universe as a free neutron.

15
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.270
So what is the answer to the question of how long it lives?

16
00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.280
For a single neutron there is no answer.

17
00:01:50.280 --> 00:02:01.130
Decay is random; a neutron does not age, does not tire, and a neutron that has already been waiting for an hour is exactly as likely to decay in the next second as one that has just been born.

18
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.460
What can be measured is the average of a crowd.

19
00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.750
That average, the mean lifetime, is about fourteen and a half minutes.

20
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:13.680
For half of a crowd of neutrons to be used up, about ten minutes is enough.

21
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:20.210
These two numbers are two different expressions of the same thing, and physicists speak of the mean lifetime.

22
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:23.860
Why is such a simple question still being asked?

23
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:30.230
Because the decay of the neutron is the cleanest, plainest example of the weak nuclear force we have in our hands.

24
00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:38.040
No accelerator is needed, no exotic particle is needed; there is only the neutron, and it falls apart of its own accord.

25
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:46.330
That is why its lifetime behaves like a constant of nature: a calibration number that goes in and out of other calculations and decides whether they are right.

26
00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:55.740
And when you are measuring a calibration number, an accuracy of one percent is not enough for you; you want one part in a thousand, even one part in ten thousand.

27
00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.950
Humanity has found two ways of measuring this, and the two of them work on exactly opposite logic.

28
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.480
The first way is the bottle.

29
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.410
You fill a vessel with neutrons, you wait, and then you count the ones left over.

30
00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:19.140
It sounds easy, but holding a neutron in a container is not easy: having no charge, it cannot be caught with electric fields, and it passes straight through walls.

31
00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.790
The solution is to slow the neutrons down to an extreme.

32
00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:32.680
Moving at a few metres a second, the pace of a walking human being, these ultracold neutrons are so feeble that gravity can hold them and a strong magnet can bounce them back.

33
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:39.370
The trap built at Los Alamos National Laboratory, in the American state of New Mexico, does exactly that:

34
00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:46.540
a bathtub-shaped vessel whose floor and sides are woven out of permanent magnets and whose lid is nothing but gravity.

35
00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.790
The neutrons never touch any material surface; they sway on a magnetic cushion.

36
00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:55.440
Fill it, wait, pour out what remains and count.

37
00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:05.970
And you do not even need an absolute number: if you compare what is left after waiting thirty minutes with what is left after waiting ninety, the slope of the exponential decline gives you the lifetime directly.

38
00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:16.340
Between two thousand seventeen and two thousand nineteen the Los Alamos team counted more than forty million neutrons this way, and in two thousand twenty-one they published this result:

39
00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:22.070
eight hundred seventy-seven point seven five seconds, with an uncertainty below half a second.

40
00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.080
Zero point zero three nine percent.

41
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.250
The most precise measurement made to date.

42
00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:32.540
And the weak point of the bottle lies in exactly the same place.

43
00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:37.790
The vessel cannot tell the difference between a neutron decaying and a neutron escaping.

44
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:48.480
Every neutron that leaks out through a gap at the edge of a wall, that warms up as it strikes and so gets free of the trap, that has its magnetic orientation flip and so escapes the magnet's push,

45
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.130
shows up in the ledger as having decayed.

46
00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:57.220
Which means every hidden loss in the bottle method makes the lifetime look shorter than it is.

47
00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.030
The second way is the beam, and it counts the dead.

48
00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:07.720
You pass a beam of cold neutrons coming out of a reactor through a volume whose length is known down to the millimetre.

49
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:18.090
Every neutron that decays inside that volume leaves a proton behind; the proton has charge, so it can be held with electric and magnetic fields and counted one by one.

50
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:28.300
Then you measure how many neutrons there are inside that same beam: you place in the beam's path a thin layer of lithium-six, which swallows a known small fraction of the neutrons going through,

51
00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.950
and from the number of absorptions you work out the flux.

52
00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:40.560
How many protons were born per second, how many neutrons were in that volume; the ratio of those two gives you the lifetime.

53
00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:49.970
The American national measurement body, the National Institute of Standards and Technology, the Institute of Standards, has been working with this method for years,

54
00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:57.460
and the result it published in two thousand thirteen was eight hundred eighty-seven point seven seconds, with an uncertainty of about two seconds.

55
00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.430
The beam's weak point, too, is the mirror of its method.

56
00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:06.680
Here you are condemned to two absolute counts: of the protons and of the neutrons.

57
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:12.410
A proton that goes unnoticed reduces the number of the dead and makes the lifetime look long.

58
00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:17.340
A neutron flux that has been overcounted does the same thing.

59
00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.110
So the errors of the beam method tend to make the lifetime look longer than it is.

60
00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.960
And here is where the collision happens.

61
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.650
The bottle says eight hundred seventy-seven point seven five; the beam says eight hundred eighty-seven point seven.

62
00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:38.020
One answer is fourteen minutes and thirty-eight seconds, the other fourteen minutes and forty-eight seconds.

63
00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:47.750
The puzzle got its name while the difference between them stood at around eight seconds; as the measurements sharpened, the difference did not close, it widened towards ten seconds.

64
00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:56.200
Put in statistical language, the probability that the two results are merely scatter around the same true value is a few parts in a thousand.

65
00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:04.490
So either there is an error in one of the two experiments that nobody has found yet, or the neutron is doing something that is not written down in our ledger.

66
00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:09.900
You might ask what difference eight or ten seconds make in a lifetime of a quarter of an hour.

67
00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.030
The difference is that this number is not a duration but a setting.

68
00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:23.440
In the first minutes of the universe the neutron's lifetime ran like an hourglass, and the rate at which that hourglass emptied determined the composition of the matter we see today.

69
00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.130
It went like this.

70
00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:34.260
About one second after the Big Bang, the universe grew too cold to go on turning protons into neutrons and neutrons into protons.

71
00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.830
At that moment a count was taken, and there was roughly one neutron for every six protons.

72
00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:43.000
But the story did not stop there.

73
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:52.090
For those neutrons to get inside a helium nucleus and reach safety, the universe had to cool enough to hold deuterium together, and that took minutes.

74
00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:56.220
Throughout those minutes free neutrons went on decaying.

75
00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:06.110
When the door to helium production finally opened, about three minutes after the explosion, every surviving neutron was locked away into helium almost instantly.

76
00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:12.480
The only reason the ratio slipped from one in six to one in seven is this clock of death, ticking all through the wait.

77
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:21.890
The fact that a quarter of the ordinary matter in the universe today is helium by mass has nothing to do with stars; that helium was laid down in those three minutes,

78
00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.540
and its quantity was written by the neutron's lifetime.

79
00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:34.790
So the calculation can be set up like this: if the neutron lives longer, more neutrons are still alive when the door opens, and there is more helium.

80
00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:38.280
Here you would want to call the universe itself as a referee.

81
00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:47.050
You look at distant primordial gas clouds that stars have not contaminated, you measure the helium fraction, and you say which lifetime it fits.

82
00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.180
Unfortunately the referee cannot yet see a difference this fine.

83
00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:58.030
A change of one percent in the lifetime shifts helium's mass fraction by only about zero point two percent.

84
00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:05.520
The ten-second dispute moves a helium fraction of around zero point two four seven by some six parts in ten thousand.

85
00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:12.050
Whereas the uncertainty on the helium fraction read off the sky is still of the order of a few parts in a thousand.

86
00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:17.780
So in this case the universe is a knowledgeable witness whose eyesight is not quite sharp enough.

87
00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:23.990
If observational sensitivity comes down to one part in a thousand, its testimony will begin to speak.

88
00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:30.680
The place where the number really hurts at the moment is elsewhere: the internal consistency of the weak force itself.

89
00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:41.530
The neutron's rate of decay is directly tied to the largest entry in the table that lists the probabilities of quarks turning into one another, the transition amplitude between the up quark and the down quark.

90
00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:49.500
If you measure the neutron's lifetime and the direction in which the electron prefers to fly off as it decays, you can calculate that entry.

91
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:56.510
Then you add up the squares of the numbers in the table's first row; theory says the total should come out to exactly one.

92
00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:06.080
That sum is one of the sharpest examinations the Standard Model faces, and in recent years an uncomfortable gap at the level of one part in a thousand has been under discussion;

93
00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:11.810
a tension that does not yet count as a discovery, but that will not go away either.

94
00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.780
The neutron could have entered this debate as an independent witness.

95
00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:23.750
It does not, because the one point one percent uncertainty in its lifetime carries a blurring of half a percent into that critical number,

96
00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:29.160
whereas the precision at which the debate is taking place is of the order of three parts in ten thousand.

97
00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.250
The marks on the ruler in your hand are twenty times thicker than the notch you need to measure.

98
00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:37.420
And now the most provocative side of the puzzle.

99
00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:42.190
What these two methods measure is not, in fact, one and the same thing.

100
00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:50.000
The bottle counts the survivors; which is to say, it is sensitive all at once to every route by which a neutron can disappear.

101
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.730
The beam counts protons; which is to say, it sees only a decay that produces a proton.

102
00:10:55.730 --> 00:11:03.060
Think about it: what would happen if about one percent of neutrons were leaving by an invisible route that produces no proton?

103
00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.830
The bottle would count those losses as decays too, and find the lifetime short.

104
00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:12.440
The beam would never see that loss at all, and find the lifetime long.

105
00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:19.290
The sign of the difference, its size, its direction; all of it sits exactly on the observed picture.

106
00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:31.100
In two thousand eighteen, Bartosz Fornal and Benjamín Grinstein put this possibility on the table as a serious proposal: perhaps the neutron, with a probability of one percent, is turning into dark matter.

107
00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:38.750
The idea was exciting, but it had to pass through a very narrow door, and nature had long since fixed the door's measurements.

108
00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:41.280
First, mass.

109
00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:52.290
The hypothetical dark particle the neutron would turn into has to be lighter than nine hundred thirty-nine point five six five megaelectronvolts; otherwise there is not enough energy for the transformation.

110
00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:59.140
But it cannot be too light either, because then the same transformation becomes possible inside nuclei as well.

111
00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:03.590
Here a quiet but decisive witness steps in: beryllium-nine.

112
00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:12.840
Among the stable nuclei, it is the one that holds on to its neutron most loosely; prising that neutron off takes only one point six six four megaelectronvolts.

113
00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:22.570
If the dark particle had a mass below that threshold, beryllium-nine nuclei would fall apart of their own accord; they would end up as two helium nuclei and a dark particle.

114
00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:26.540
Beryllium-nine is still lying around, not falling apart.

115
00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:37.070
So the dark particle's mass is squeezed into a window between nine hundred thirty-seven point nine and nine hundred thirty-nine point six, a window not even two megaelectronvolts wide.

116
00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:44.080
Before the hypothesis was even born, the mere existence of a stable nucleus had closed off most of the possibilities.

117
00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:46.770
Then came the turn of evidence.

118
00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:57.460
If the neutron emits a photon alongside as it turns into a dark particle, that photon's energy has to lie between seven hundred eighty-two and sixteen hundred sixty-four kiloelectronvolts;

119
00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:03.990
the lower bound is set by the energy budget of ordinary decay, the upper bound by beryllium's loose grip.

120
00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:07.640
So the place to search was marked out to the centimetre.

121
00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:14.010
The Los Alamos team arranged sensitive detectors around the neutron trap and looked at precisely that range.

122
00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.700
Not a single line.

123
00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:21.950
A photon channel at the one percent level was ruled out with ninety-nine percent confidence.

124
00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:26.880
And what if an electron-positron pair comes out alongside instead?

125
00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:38.690
Another experiment in the same laboratory, measuring the electrons from neutron decay, excluded such a channel with a total energy above one hundred kiloelectronvolts at a certainty well beyond five sigma.

126
00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:48.260
A team working at the Grenoble research reactor in France also ruled out, independently, a one percent contribution across ninety-five percent of the permitted mass window.

127
00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:51.590
So the visible versions of dark decay are dead.

128
00:13:51.590 --> 00:14:00.200
What is left standing is a channel that leaves no trace at all, wholly dark: a dark particle plus a dark carrier, zero signal in the laboratory.

129
00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:05.930
Searching for this directly is hardly possible, but it is not entirely unconstrained either.

130
00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:19.660
If neutrons can turn into a dark particle, that transformation works best where neutrons are most abundant, inside neutron stars; it changes the star's internal pressure, its structure, the greatest mass it can bear.

131
00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:27.310
The neutron stars we observe, reaching twice the mass of the Sun, therefore place an indirect but real limit on the hypothesis.

132
00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:36.240
It has to be said plainly here: dark decay is not a proven explanation today, only a possibility that has not yet been completely eliminated.

133
00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:45.650
Meanwhile the boring explanation, the possibility that there is a systematic error in one of the experiments, has gained strength from an unexpected quarter.

134
00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:53.780
At Japan's accelerator facility J-PARC, the Japan Proton Accelerator Research Complex, a third approach has been set up.

135
00:14:53.780 --> 00:15:02.390
This apparatus also uses a cold neutron beam, but instead of the protons it counts the electrons that come out of the decay inside a gas chamber,

136
00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:09.880
and it measures the number of neutrons in the beam from the reactions given by helium-three nuclei mixed into that same gas.

137
00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:15.610
The same question, an entirely different geometry, an entirely different family of errors.

138
00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:24.700
The result that came out of the data the Japanese centre collected between two thousand fourteen and two thousand twenty-three was announced at the end of two thousand twenty-four:

139
00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:28.190
eight hundred seventy-seven point two seconds.

140
00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:36.960
Its statistical uncertainty is one point seven seconds, its systematic uncertainty still around four seconds, so it is not as sharp as the bottle.

141
00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:44.450
But what it points to is clear: it agrees with the bottle, and it stands in tension with the average of the beams that count protons.

142
00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:47.620
This changes the geography of the puzzle.

143
00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:53.670
The fault line may not be beam against bottle at all, but only in the proton trap's own accounting.

144
00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:57.480
That is why the next step was not to argue but to build.

145
00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:02.570
The beam experiment at the Institute of Standards is being rebuilt from the ground up;

146
00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:11.020
the goal is to test every known systematic effect at the level of one part in ten thousand and to push the final uncertainty far below one second.

147
00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:15.310
The answer from this experiment could close the puzzle from either direction.

148
00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:25.840
If the new beam result comes down to around eight hundred seventy-seven, the eight-second difference will quietly go on the record as a measurement error, and nobody will go on hunting for a dark particle.

149
00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:37.490
If instead it brings its uncertainty down to half a second and still insists on eight hundred eighty-eight, the idea that the neutron has an invisible escape route turns into a serious candidate for a discovery.

150
00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:46.580
What this puzzle really teaches is perhaps this: when two careful measurements fail to agree, physics does not vote on which of them is the more likeable.

151
00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:55.350
It publishes the difference openly, it writes down every single time which method was used to measure the lifetime, and it builds a third instrument to close the gap.

152
00:16:55.350 --> 00:17:04.600
The neutron is the simplest unstable particle we can hold in our hands; there is nothing mysterious about its quark structure, nothing complicated about its decay scheme.

153
00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:10.010
And even so, we cannot say how long it lives to one part in a thousand.

154
00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:18.940
Countless neutrons are passing through the room you are in right now, every second, and every one of them knows better than we do how many seconds it has before it falls apart.

155
00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:22.590
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

156
00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:26.240
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
